// Trax Combat Enhancements - DEFAULTS (defaults.json)
//
// The one place every setting's default lives. The build embeds this file in the mod: a first
// run writes these values into config.json, a key missing from config.json falls back to them,
// and MCM's Reset and "Revert all to defaults" restore them.
//
// To change a default: edit the value after the colon (true/false, or a number with a dot:
// 0.75), save, build and deploy. Keep every key - the tests check each value's type and range.
//
// The // lines are written by the mod from its list of settings. When a setting's wording,
// range or place changes in the code, rewrite them (every value is kept) from the repo root:
//     dotnet run --project tools/DefaultsTool -- refresh
//
// The game never reads this file for a player's own values - those live in config.json in
// Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\TraxCombatEnhancements\
// MCM's "Save current values as a defaults file" writes the values in play as a file like this
// one, next to config.json - copy it over the repo's defaults.json to make them the defaults.
{
  // ######## THE MAIN SETTINGS - MCM page "Trax Combat Enhancements" ########

  // ==================== Master switch ====================
  // Turn the whole mod off and every battle is plain vanilla: no damage rolls or reduction, no
  // Athletics, no pauses between attacks, no step backs, no bars. The log still records each
  // battle (its summary says mod ON or OFF), so the same battle can be fought both ways and
  // compared. Switched back on, everyone starts with a full Athletics bar.
  // (true or false)
  "ModEnabled": true,

  // ==================== How hard hits land ====================
  // Every hit that lands deals a random share of its normal damage - this far up or down,
  // rolled fresh on every hit. 50: a 50-damage hit lands for anything from 25 to 75. 0 = no
  // roll. Which hits roll: Advanced page, Damage.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "DamageRandomPercent": 50,

  // Every hit deals this much less damage, after the random roll - on people, horses and
  // shields alike, from any weapon, arrow, kick or horse charge, and whether the roll is on or
  // off. 25: an average 100-damage hit lands for 75, so it takes about a third more hits to
  // bring a man down. A hit never drops below 1. Falls and hits on doors and siege engines keep
  // the game's damage. 0 = the game's damage.
  // (range 0 to 90)
  "DamageReductionPercent": 25,

  // ==================== Tiredness (Athletics) ====================
  // On: every fighter has an Athletics bar - his stamina, as big as his Athletics skill - that
  // his attacks and blocks drain and rest refills. At or above the peak line (the white mark)
  // he fights at full strength; below it the top of his damage roll, his attack speed and his
  // run speed fall - down to long pauses between attacks and a slow run when it is empty - and
  // tired AI fighters step back. Off: no Athletics at all - no costs, no slowing, no step
  // backs, no bars (and no sprint, aggression by tiredness or staggers).
  // (true or false)
  "AthleticsEnabled": true,

  // How fast men tire: the Athletics points one attack costs a common soldier, whatever the
  // size of his bar - so a bigger Athletics skill means more blows. At 7 a recruit (a 50-point
  // bar) is empty after 8 blows, a legionary (130) after 19. Higher = everyone tires sooner. 0
  // = attacks are free. Kicks, bashes and blocks have their own costs: Advanced page,
  // Athletics.
  // (range 0.0 to 100.0)
  "CostPerBlow": 7.0,

  // Heroes (lords, companions and you) pay every Athletics cost - blows, kicks, bashes, blocks
  // - times this, so they last longer than common soldiers. 0.75 = a quarter less. 1 = the same
  // as anyone.
  // (range 0.0 to 2.0)
  "HeroCostMultiplier": 0.75,

  // ==================== How the AI fights ====================
  // How eagerly an AI soldier on foot with a FULL Athletics bar decides to start an attack (and
  // to strike back after a parry), in percent of the game's own AI. It slides in a straight
  // line to the next setting as his bar empties - the more tired he is, the less he swings. 60:
  // a charging man's 14% chance becomes 8.6%. Tournaments and duels too. Never you, never
  // riders (their swings come with each pass), never archers' shooting. 100 with the next at
  // 100 = the game's own AI.
  // (range 0 to 200)
  "AiAggressionFreshPercent": 60,

  // The same at an EMPTY bar: 20 = a fifth of the game's eagerness (a charging man's 14%
  // becomes 2.9%). A wounded man's bar is capped by his wounds, so he is less eager too. On top
  // of the pause after each attack - a tired man has both brakes.
  // (range 0 to 200)
  "AiAggressionExhaustedPercent": 20,

  // When an AI formation gets a NEW charge or advance order - yours or its own general's - each
  // of its soldiers on foot gets a FEVER: for a while he attacks with a surge of eagerness,
  // then calms down again. The fever's clock starts only when he first comes near an enemy. A
  // hold, move or retreat order ends it. Never you, never riders; tournaments and duels have no
  // orders, so no fever. Off: every man's eagerness follows his bar alone. How strong and how
  // long: Advanced page, Aggression and fever.
  // (true or false)
  "FeverEnabled": true,

  // AI soldiers on foot in a shield wall, square or circle keep their place in the formation
  // instead of stepping out of it at the enemy they fight - they still fight whatever reaches
  // them, so the wall stays a wall. Under any order but a charge or a retreat; a man far behind
  // his place may still run to rejoin. Both sides, never you, never riders, a siege's attackers
  // or naval battles. Off: the game's own way - each man may walk a few steps out at his enemy
  // and back.
  // (true or false)
  "ShieldWallHoldsSlots": true,

  // With "Shield walls hold their slots" on: a held soldier may step up to this many metres out
  // of his place at an enemy close in front of it (one who backed off just out of reach),
  // strike, then he is held again and walks back to his place - he may step out again once he
  // is back. 0 = no step at all: they stand fast and fight only what reaches them. Does nothing
  // while "hold their slots" is off.
  // (range 0.0 to 3.0)
  "ShieldWallStepOutMetres": 1.0,

  // Every AI soldier on foot in a formation weighs his mates against the enemies near him - by
  // how tough each one is (armour, level, wounds). Short of courage he is TIMID: far from his
  // mates he walks back to a bigger group of his side, facing the enemy (sprinting when it is
  // far); among them he holds and strikes only what comes within reach - no lone man circling
  // your shield wall, no exhausted man standing among four enemies. A big enough crowd stays
  // brave and wraps around a line as a mass. Never you, never riders, never men held in a
  // shield wall. Works without the Athletics system (then tiredness does not count). Off: the
  // game's own bravery - the log still measures, so flipping it mid-battle compares the two.
  // (true or false)
  "CourageEnabled": true,

  // AI riders on a charge stop going in one by one. A fresh, eager rider charges again and
  // again as in the game; the more tired he gets, the more he looks for riders of his own kind
  // in his formation - horse archers with horse archers, skirmishers with skirmishers, lancers
  // with lancers - and the bigger the group he wants before he attacks again: 4-5 when half
  // tired, most of his kind when exhausted. The flock never sits: it keeps riding, drifting
  // toward the unit's centre while its riders are tired and bending toward the enemy as its
  // most rested riders lead, and when it is big enough it charges together, as a wave. Both
  // sides, never you; only riders whose formation is on a CHARGE (hold, move, follow and
  // advance keep them in their slots). Works without courage. Off: the game's own riders - the
  // log still measures, so flipping it mid-battle compares the two.
  // (true or false)
  "CavalryFlockEnabled": true,

  // ==================== Staggers and knockdowns ====================
  // Kicks, shield bashes, blocks and clean hits can stagger a man, make him drop his shield or
  // knock him down - more likely the more tired he is and the more he is outmatched (both men's
  // CURRENT Athletics, the weapon and its swing, the guard, both men's skill). Two fresh, even
  // men: never. Needs the Athletics system on. Off: the game's own reactions only. Each kind on
  // its own and every chance: Advanced page, Impact.
  // (true or false)
  "ImpactEnabled": true,

  // Every chance of a stagger, a dropped shield or a knockdown against YOU is this percent of
  // the AI's. 100 = the same rules as everyone; 0 = you are never staggered, disarmed of your
  // shield or knocked down by it.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "ImpactOnPlayerPercent": 100,

  // ==================== Sprinting ====================
  // Every fighter on foot has a sprint: a short burst of speed stored in seconds - the more
  // Athletics he has left, the longer (150 points = 7.5 s; empty = none). Hold the sprint key
  // to run faster; the AI sprints when its formation gets a new charge or advance order. While
  // sprinting his Athletics does not refill. Never on a horse. Off: no sprint, speeds back to
  // normal at once. How fast and how long: Advanced page, Sprint.
  // (true or false)
  "SprintEnabled": true,

  // Hold this key to sprint (on foot, in a fight). Type its name: LeftControl, RightControl,
  // LeftAlt, RightAlt, LeftShift, RightShift, CapsLock, Tab, Q, T, Y, H, J, K, L, B, N, M, C,
  // MiddleMouseButton, X1MouseButton, X2MouseButton. Left Control is free in the game's default
  // keys (Left Shift is zoom, Z crouch, Caps Lock walk, Left Alt the formation markers).
  // (one of LeftControl, RightControl, LeftAlt, RightAlt, LeftShift, RightShift, CapsLock, Tab, Q, T, Y, H, J, K, L, B, N, M, C, MiddleMouseButton, X1MouseButton, X2MouseButton)
  "SprintKey": "LeftControl",

  // ==================== On screen ====================
  // Your Athletics bar, bottom right under your health bar: the Athletics you have left and
  // your whole bar ("132 / 180"), a white mark at the peak line, the fill in the colour of your
  // strength (green at full strength, then blue, yellow, orange, red) and the part your wounds
  // hold shown dark. The attack recovery and sprint bars above it show with it.
  // (true or false)
  "ShowPlayerBar": true,

  // While you hold ALT (or the orders menu is open), small Athletics and fever bars float above
  // the soldiers. Off (the default): only on the few men you look at - the ones nearest the
  // centre of the screen and close to the camera; the formations' averages are under their
  // markers. On: every man on the screen gets his bars (at most a cap, the nearest first). How
  // many and how far: Advanced page, Hold ALT.
  // (true or false)
  "AltMarksEveryone": false,

  // A short line in the game's message feed when YOU are knocked down, knocked off your horse,
  // lose your shield or reel from a riposte - and when you knock someone down, off his horse,
  // break his guard or knock his shield away. At most one line every few seconds, several
  // things at once merged into one. Never in shadow mode (nothing really happens there).
  // (true or false)
  "ImpactShowMessages": true,

  // ######## ADVANCED - for tuning: MCM page "Trax Combat Enhancements - Advanced" ########

  // ==================== Damage ====================
  // On: every hit that lands deals a random share of its normal damage (the Damage roll on the
  // main page). Off: no roll - every hit deals the game's own damage (less the damage
  // reduction); the rest of the mod still runs.
  // (true or false)
  "DamageRandomEnabled": true,

  // Randomize hits by swords, axes, maces, spears and fists, and horse-charge bumps.
  // (true or false)
  "DamageRandomMelee": true,

  // Randomize hits by arrows, bolts, sling stones and thrown weapons.
  // (true or false)
  "DamageRandomRanged": true,

  // Also randomize hits that land on horses and other mounts.
  // (true or false)
  "DamageRandomOnMounts": true,

  // Also randomize the damage a shield takes when it blocks a blow. Off: shields wear down at
  // the game's usual pace (less the damage reduction below).
  // (true or false)
  "DamageRandomOnShields": false,

  // Step 62: a man who CHARGES - sprinting into the blow, or his first blow just after a sprint
  // - rolls the whole damage bonus however tired he is (the bonus otherwise shrinks with his
  // Athletics). Off: his tiredness counts as for any blow.
  // (true or false)
  "SprintFullCritical": true,

  // ==================== Athletics ====================
  // No fighter's Athletics bar is smaller than this, whatever his Athletics skill - so a
  // recruit with Athletics skill 20 still gets a 50-point bar (5 blows). 0 = no floor: the bar
  // is exactly the skill (never below 1 point). Changed mid-battle, everyone keeps his share: a
  // fighter at 60% stays at 60%.
  // (range 0 to 1000)
  "AthleticsPoolFloor": 50,

  // Athletics points per point of Athletics skill. 1.0: the bar tops at the skill - skill 180,
  // a 180-point bar. Changed mid-battle, everyone keeps his share.
  // (range 0.1 to 5.0)
  "AthleticsPoolPerSkill": 1.0,

  // Where the peak line sits - the white mark on the bar - in percent of each fighter's own
  // bar. At or above it he is at full strength: full damage upside, attack speed and run speed.
  // Below it each of those falls in a straight line to its lowest value at an empty bar. 100 =
  // only a full bar is full strength.
  // (range 10 to 100)
  "AthleticsPeakPercent": 75,

  // On: a wounded fighter can only use the share of his bar that matches the health he has left
  // - at 75% health, 75% of the bar; the rest is cut at once and does not refill while the
  // wound lasts. The peak line stays where it was, so a badly wounded fighter never gets back
  // to full strength.
  // (true or false)
  "HealthCapsAthletics": true,

  // Athletics points one kick or one shield bash costs a common soldier - yours and the AI's
  // alike. Heroes pay it times the hero cost and party leaders times both, just like a blow: 3
  // costs a hero 2.25 and a party leader about 1.7. Paid once, when the kick or bash starts,
  // landed or not. It never starts the pause between attacks. 0 = kicks and bashes are free.
  // (range 0.0 to 20.0)
  "CostPerKickOrBash": 3.0,

  // Athletics points a common soldier pays for each melee blow he blocks with his shield held
  // on the right side - yours and the AI's alike, on foot or mounted. Heroes pay it times the
  // hero cost and party leaders times both, like a blow. Paid once per blocked blow; it does
  // not stop your refill unless the switch below is off. Blocking is never held or slowed.
  // Arrows and bolts stopped by a shield are free. 0 = free.
  // (range 0.0 to 20.0)
  "CostPerShieldBlock": 1.0,

  // Athletics points a common soldier pays for each melee blow his shield stops while held to
  // the wrong side - the blow came from another direction than his guard, and the game counts
  // it as a clumsy block. Heroes and party leaders pay less, like a blow. 0 = free.
  // (range 0.0 to 20.0)
  "CostPerWrongSideShieldBlock": 2.0,

  // Athletics points a common soldier pays for each melee blow he blocks or parries with his
  // weapon (no shield) - a chamber block too. Heroes and party leaders pay less, like a blow. 0
  // = free.
  // (range 0.0 to 20.0)
  "CostPerWeaponParry": 2.0,

  // On: a shield block on the right side and a weapon parry still cost their points, but the
  // bar keeps refilling underneath - a good blocker can win his breath back while he stays on
  // the defence. Only a block with the shield held to the wrong side (and your own attacks,
  // kicks and bashes) restarts the wait before the refill. Off: every paid block restarts that
  // wait, so a man under constant attack never refills.
  // (true or false)
  "OnlyWrongSideBlocksStopRefill": true,

  // On: every attack costs Athletics, landed or not. Off: only attacks that hit something (a
  // body, a shield, a parrying weapon) cost.
  // (true or false)
  "CostOnMiss": true,

  // The hero who leads the fighter's own party (you for your party, a lord for his) pays times
  // this again, on top of the hero price: 10 × 0.75 × 0.75 = about 5.6 per blow.
  // (range 0.0 to 2.0)
  "PartyLeaderCostMultiplier": 0.75,

  // ==================== Tired fighters ====================
  // How often a fighter with an empty bar can attack, in percent of normal. His attacks play at
  // full speed; after each one he must wait before he can start the next: the attack's own
  // length times (100 / this - 1) - at 20 he waits four attack lengths, so he attacks once
  // where a fresh man attacks five times. From the peak line down to empty it falls in a
  // straight line. 100 = attacks never slow down. Blocking is never slowed.
  // (range 5 to 100)
  "ExhaustedAttackSpeedPercent": 20,

  // On: below the peak line, after each of your attacks your attack button does nothing until
  // the pause is over - the Attack recovery bar above your Athletics bar fills back up
  // meanwhile. Keep the button held and your next attack starts the moment it is full.
  // Blocking, kicks, moving and switching weapons always work. Off: your own attacks are never
  // held (the AI's still are). Off mid-battle: your pause ends at once.
  // (true or false)
  "AttackRatePlayerTimer": true,

  // On: after each attack - melee or ranged, on foot or mounted - a tired AI fighter does not
  // start another until his pause is over; his guard stays up. The "Battle pace (AI)" waits
  // ride on it too. Off: no AI fighter is held - neither the tired pause nor the battle-pace
  // waits. Off mid-battle: every waiting fighter may attack again at once.
  // (true or false)
  "AttackRatePaceHold": true,

  // On: a tired AI fighter waiting out his pause keeps fighting in every way but one - only his
  // wish to attack is taken out of his own controls, so he still blocks, parries and moves as
  // he likes. Off: the old way - the game's own 'no attack' order, under which waiting men
  // blocked almost nothing (2-13% in the playtest). An A/B switch: the battle summary's guard
  // line compares the two. A pause already running finishes the way it began.
  // (true or false)
  "AttackRatePaceByInput": true,

  // On: when a tired AI fighter who is waiting out his pause or stepping back wants to attack,
  // he raises his guard instead (a block - with a shield, the shield). Off: his attack is
  // simply dropped and he blocks only when his own AI decides to. Only with the new ways
  // ("Tired AI keep their guard up", "Step back = walk backwards").
  // (true or false)
  "AiHoldRaiseGuard": true,

  // On: a tired AI fighter also decides to attack (and to strike back after a parry) less
  // often, looses arrows less readily and aims longer - by his attack speed - on top of the
  // pause. Off: the pause alone slows him. With both on they stack and tired AI fighters attack
  // slower than asked, so it is off unless you want that.
  // (true or false)
  "AttackRateAiDecisions": false,

  // How fast the attack animations - wind-up and swing, thrust, bow draw, throw, crossbow
  // reload - play for a fighter with an empty bar, in percent of normal. From the peak line
  // down to empty they slow in a straight line (at 85: full speed at the peak line, 92.5%
  // halfway, 85% empty), so a tired man's swings look a little heavier. The pause between
  // attacks stays the same in seconds - a slower swing only adds its own extra moment. 100 =
  // always full speed: the whole slow-down is the pause. With "Attack speed when empty" at 100
  // they never slow either. Run speed is not affected.
  // (range 5 to 100)
  "AttackAnimationMinPercent": 85,

  // On: every fighter stores swings - one per 20 Athletics points he has now (see below), never
  // fewer than 2. Each attack spends one and needs no wait while one is left; with none left he
  // waits out the pause as before. One comes back per pause length while he is not attacking,
  // so he attacks just as often on average - but he can save swings up and strike several in a
  // row. Your Attack recovery bar shows the count. Off: the pause after every attack below the
  // peak line (the old way).
  // (true or false)
  "SwingStackEnabled": true,

  // The swing stack holds his CURRENT Athletics points divided by this, rounded (7.5 makes 8):
  // a full 150 bar holds 8, half of it 4, a full 50 bar 3. Lower = more stored swings.
  // (range 5 to 100)
  "SwingStackPointsPerSwing": 20,

  // The swing stack never holds fewer places than this, however low his Athletics - even empty
  // he can save up this many swings.
  // (range 1 to 10)
  "SwingStackMinimum": 2,

  // On (with the swing stack): a kick or a shield bash spends a stored swing like an attack,
  // and with none left it cannot start - your kick key does nothing until one is back. It still
  // never starts a pause by itself. Off: kicks and bashes are free of the stack (they still
  // cost their Athletics).
  // (true or false)
  "KicksUseSwings": true,

  // Top speed on foot of a fighter with an empty bar, times his normal top speed. From the peak
  // line down to empty it falls in a straight line, so fresh men overtake tired ones. 1.0 =
  // tired men run as fast as fresh ones.
  // (range 0.1 to 1.0)
  "MinMoveSpeedMultiplier": 0.3,

  // Top speed of a horse whose rider's bar is empty, times its normal top speed, on the same
  // straight line. 1.0 = horses never slow down, however tired the rider.
  // (range 0.1 to 1.0)
  "MountMinSpeedMultiplier": 1.0,

  // On: the lucky side of the damage roll shrinks as the attacker tires - at or above the peak
  // line a hit can land up to +50% (with Spread at 50), halfway down to empty up to +25%, empty
  // never above normal. The unlucky side never changes. Off: every attacker gets the full roll.
  // (true or false)
  "DamageBonusFollowsAthletics": true,

  // How much a tired AI fighter's block direction is left to chance. At or above the peak line
  // his own AI picks it. Below it, this share times how far down he is becomes a random pick
  // instead: at 100, halfway down half his blocks go in a random direction, and an empty man
  // guesses every block - right only one time in four, by luck. A wrong guess with a shield is
  // a wrong-side block (it costs more and stops his refill); with a weapon the blow lands. It
  // raises the game's own chance for clumsy fighters, never lowers it. On foot and mounted;
  // never you. 0 = off: the game's own blocking.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "TiredBlockRandomPercent": 100,

  // ==================== Tired fighters step back ====================
  // On: after a melee swing, a tired AI fighter on foot may step back a little, facing his
  // enemy with his guard up, then return to his place in the formation - so the tired fall back
  // and the fresh take the blows. Never you, never riders, never after a shot or a throw; field
  // battles only. Off mid-battle: everyone stepping back returns to his formation at once.
  // (true or false)
  "StepBackEnabled": true,

  // On: a fighter stepping back walks backwards, like you holding S - he keeps facing his enemy
  // with his guard up and stops when he has covered the step distance or the step time runs out
  // (or the ground behind him ends). Off: the old way - the game walks him to a spot behind
  // him, and most men turned their backs to do it (80% in the playtest). An A/B switch: the
  // summary's facing and guard lines compare the two. A step back already running finishes the
  // way it began.
  // (true or false)
  "StepBackBackpedal": true,

  // Chance, in percent, that a fighter with an empty bar steps back after a melee swing. It
  // falls in a straight line to 0 at the peak line: halfway down, half this chance; at full
  // strength never. 0 = nobody steps back.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "StepBackMaxChancePercent": 100,

  // How far back a fighter steps, in metres, straight away from the enemy he fights. A tired
  // man walks slowly, so he may not get all the way before the step time runs out.
  // (range 0.5 to 5.0)
  "StepBackDistance": 2.0,

  // How long a step back lasts at most, in seconds. Then his formation takes him again and he
  // walks back to his place.
  // (range 0.3 to 5.0)
  "StepBackSeconds": 1.5,

  // A fighter steps back only while the enemy he fights is at most this many metres away - with
  // nobody close there is nothing to step back from.
  // (range 1.0 to 20.0)
  "StepBackEnemyRange": 4.0,

  // On: a fighter stepping back does not attack - guard up only - until he is back with his
  // formation. Off: he may still strike while he backs away.
  // (true or false)
  "StepBackHoldAttacks": true,

  // At most this many fighters, on all sides together, step back at the same time. It keeps big
  // battles cheap and stops a whole front line from stepping back together.
  // (range 1 to 1000)
  "StepBackMaxAtOnce": 50,

  // ==================== Battle pace (AI) ====================
  // AI soldiers on foot fighting with a shield in their other hand swing this many percent less
  // often than they otherwise would, fresh or tired: after each swing they wait a little
  // longer, guard up, before the next. 30 = 7 swings where they made 10. Read at every swing
  // from what he really holds - heroes and lords too; never you, never riders. Rides on "Tired
  // AI wait out the pause" (off there = no AI pause at all). 0 = off. A change applies at each
  // fighter's next swing.
  // (range 0 to 90)
  "ShieldInfantrySwingsLessPercent": 30,

  // The same for AI soldiers on foot fighting WITHOUT a shield - two-handed swords and axes,
  // polearms, a one-hander alone: this many percent fewer swings, fresh or tired. 0 = off. A
  // change applies at each fighter's next swing.
  // (range 0 to 90)
  "FootMeleeSwingsLessPercent": 15,

  // How long an AI soldier on foot at full strength waits on his own between the end of one
  // swing and the start of the next - about 1 second in the playtests. The two settings above
  // size their wait on his whole rhythm (his swing, his tired pause and this gap), so that he
  // really swings that percent less. The battle summary's "battle pace" lines show his real gap
  // after a wait: raise this if it reads longer, lower it if shorter.
  // (range 0.0 to 5.0)
  "AiMeleeGapSeconds": 1.0,

  // AI bowmen - on foot and horse archers alike - wait this many seconds more after each shot,
  // on top of any tired pause. At 2.0 they fire about 30% fewer arrows a minute (a fresh AI
  // bowman shot about once every 4.5 seconds in the playtests). Never you. 0 = off. A change
  // applies at each archer's next shot.
  // (range 0.0 to 10.0)
  "ExtraPauseAfterBowShotSeconds": 2.0,

  // AI crossbowmen wait this many seconds more after each shot, on top of any tired pause. At
  // 2.5 they fire about 30% fewer bolts a minute (a fresh AI crossbowman shoots about once
  // every 6 seconds, the long reload included). Thrown weapons and slings have no extra wait.
  // Never you. 0 = off. A change applies at each crossbowman's next shot.
  // (range 0.0 to 10.0)
  "ExtraPauseAfterCrossbowShotSeconds": 2.5,

  // ==================== Brace by orders (AI) ====================
  // An AI soldier whose Athletics bar falls to his formation's order floor (the settings below)
  // stops attacking in melee and only defends, guard up, until he refills to that floor + the
  // recover margin. Bows, crossbows and throws go on. AI heroes and riders too; never you. Off:
  // AI men swing at any Athletics. Switching it off lifts every brace at once.
  // (true or false)
  "BraceEnabled": true,

  // Under a CHARGE order (charge, charge a target, the AI's own charge, attacking a gate) a
  // soldier braces when his Athletics bar is at or below this % of his full bar. 0 = only when
  // empty. The new floor applies at once when his formation's order changes.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "BraceFloorChargePercent": 20,

  // Under an ADVANCE order (yours, or the AI's own advance into the enemy) a soldier braces at
  // or below this % of his full bar.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "BraceFloorAdvancePercent": 40,

  // Under every other order - hold / halt, move to a position, retreat, fall back, follow, no
  // order - a soldier braces at or below this % of his full bar. High = a holding line swings
  // first and then braces early.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "BraceFloorHoldPercent": 60,

  // A soldier in no formation - tournament and arena fighters, duels - braces at or below this
  // % of his full bar. Low = a duel opponent fights on until he is nearly spent, instead of
  // turtling behind his shield.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "BraceFloorNoFormationPercent": 20,

  // Each AI soldier's own floor is his order's floor plus or minus up to this many points,
  // rolled once per soldier and battle (apart from the margin's roll below) - some brace
  // sooner, some fight on longer. Never below 0 or above 100. 0 = everyone exactly on his
  // order's floor. A change rescales everyone's roll at once.
  // (range 0 to 30)
  "BraceFloorSpreadPercent": 10,

  // A bracing soldier attacks again once his bar is back to his floor + this many points (60 +
  // 20 = 80%). A wound caps it: he stops bracing at the most his wounds let him refill to, so a
  // wounded man is never stuck. Blocks cost Athletics and stop the refill, so a man pressed
  // hard may brace a long time.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "BraceRecoverPercent": 20,

  // Each AI soldier's own margin is the setting above plus or minus up to this many points,
  // rolled once per soldier and battle - some come back sooner, some later (bravery). 0 =
  // everyone exactly the margin above. A change rescales everyone's roll at once; his target is
  // never below his floor + 1.
  // (range 0 to 50)
  "BraceRecoverSpreadPercent": 5,

  // When a soldier starts bracing and carries a shield that is not in his hand, he takes it out
  // (and a one-handed weapon first if his weapon needs both hands). A man with a bow or
  // crossbow in hand is left alone. When the brace ends his own AI picks his weapons again.
  // (true or false)
  "BraceWieldShield": true,

  // While bracing with a shield in hand a soldier holds it up whenever his own AI has no guard
  // up (his own blocks and parries stay his). Off: the guard is raised only when he wants to
  // attack.
  // (true or false)
  "BraceRaiseShield": true,

  // ==================== Sprint ====================
  // The sprint store holds his CURRENT Athletics points divided by this, in seconds: 150 points
  // = 7.5 s at 20, 50 points = 2.5 s. When his points drop (a blow, a wound) the stored seconds
  // drop with them. Lower = longer sprints.
  // (range 5.0 to 100.0)
  "SprintPointsPerSecond": 20.0,

  // The sprint store is never smaller than this, however tired he is: an exhausted man (an
  // empty Athletics bar) still holds 2 s of sprint - a dodge - it just refills slower. Above it
  // the store is his points divided by the points per second. 0 = no floor (an empty bar cannot
  // sprint).
  // (range 0.0 to 5.0)
  "SprintMinSeconds": 2.0,

  // A sprint starts only with at least this percent of the store filled - no spamming tiny
  // bursts. 0 = any amount.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "SprintMinStartPercent": 20,

  // The sprint's top speed, times his FRESH run speed (as if untired) - whatever his Athletics:
  // 2.0 = twice a fresh man's run, for a tired man too. A sprint from a half-full store starts
  // at this speed too.
  // (range 1.0 to 3.0)
  "SprintMaxMultiplier": 2.0,

  // After the full-speed part the speed falls in a straight line to this times his CURRENT run
  // speed (after tiredness) at the sprint's end: a fresh man ends at 1.1 x his run, an
  // exhausted one at 1.1 x his slow run. Above the start speed = the start speed all the way.
  // (range 1.0 to 3.0)
  "SprintMinMultiplier": 1.1,

  // The first this-percent of a sprint's length run at the start speed, then it falls to the
  // end speed. A sprint is as long as the seconds stored when it began: a full 7.5 s sprint
  // runs 2.25 s at full speed at 30. 100 = full speed all the way.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "SprintFullSpeedSharePercent": 30,

  // While sprinting a man reaches his top speed in at most this many seconds (the game's own on
  // foot is about 2.3-2.8 s - longer than a short sprint's full-speed part). Back to the game's
  // value when the sprint ends. 0 = the game's own acceleration.
  // (range 0.0 to 5.0)
  "SprintTopSpeedReachSeconds": 1.0,

  // Seconds to refill an empty sprint store to full while standing still or walking at full
  // strength (at or above the peak line). It refills only while he is not sprinting.
  // (range 1.0 to 120.0)
  "SprintRefillSeconds": 7.0,

  // The sprint store refills at this times its rate while he moves faster than a walk (0.5 =
  // half: 14 s instead of 7). 1 = moving refills as fast as standing.
  // (range 0.0 to 1.0)
  "SprintRefillMovingFactor": 0.5,

  // Below the peak line the sprint store refills slower, in a straight line down to this times
  // its rate at an empty bar (0.5 = half). It multiplies with the running factor: running and
  // half tired at 0.5 / 0.5 = 0.375 of the rate.
  // (range 0.0 to 1.0)
  "SprintRefillExhaustedFactor": 0.5,

  // When an AI formation gets a NEW movement order - yours or its own general's - each of its
  // soldiers on foot sprints once: everything he holds above that order's reserve below (a
  // charge all of it, an advance down to half, a move only a little). He stops early once an
  // enemy is within a few metres or the order changes again. Hold, retreat, fall back and
  // follow never start one. Never you, never a hero you drive.
  // (true or false)
  "SprintAiEnabled": true,

  // On a new CHARGE order (charge - F1 F3 -, charge a target, the AI general's own charge) each
  // AI soldier on foot keeps this percent of his FULL sprint store for later orders and sprints
  // everything above it. 0 = a charge sprints all of it; 100 = no sprint on a charge.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "SprintAiChargeKeepPercent": 0,

  // On a new ADVANCE order (advance / engage - F1 F4 -, or the AI general's own advance) each
  // AI soldier on foot keeps this percent of his FULL sprint store for later orders and sprints
  // everything above it. 50: from a full store he sprints half, from 60% a tenth, at or below
  // half none.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "SprintAiAdvanceKeepPercent": 50,

  // On a new MOVE order (move to a position - F1 F1 -, or an AI general's move that is neither
  // its advance nor its charge) each AI soldier on foot keeps this percent of his FULL sprint
  // store for later orders and sprints everything above it - so troops moved into position
  // still have their sprint for the real charge. 80: from a full store he sprints a fifth, from
  // 90% a tenth, at or below 80% none.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "SprintAiMoveKeepPercent": 80,

  // When an AI soldier on foot breaks and ROUTS (the game's own rout - his morale gone), he
  // spends his WHOLE sprint store once, at the rout's start, for the first get-away (no order
  // reserve, it does not end near an enemy). The rout itself stays the game's - only the speed
  // is added. Needs the AI sprint on. Off: routing men run at their normal tired speed.
  // (true or false)
  "SprintOnRout": true,

  // ==================== Aggression and fever (AI) ====================
  // A man's fever lasts his CURRENT Athletics points divided by this, in seconds: 100 points =
  // 10 s at 10, a tired man at 40 points only 4 s. Veterans have bigger Athletics pools, so
  // they surge longer; tired men surge shorter. Lower = longer fevers. Read when the order
  // comes.
  // (range 1.0 to 50.0)
  "FeverPointsPerSecond": 10.0,

  // In his fever a man decides to attack this eagerly, in percent of the game's own AI,
  // whatever his bar - but never less than his tired eagerness. It fades in a straight line
  // back to his tired eagerness as his fever burns down. At or below the full-bar setting the
  // fever adds nothing to a fresh man.
  // (range 0 to 200)
  "FeverAggressionPercent": 80,

  // The fever's clock starts the first time an enemy comes within this many metres of him - a
  // general orders the charge from afar, and the fever should not be spent on the walk. Once
  // burning it burns on, enemy near or not, until it is empty.
  // (range 1.0 to 20.0)
  "FeverContactMetres": 5.0,

  // While a man holds a shield in his other hand his fever burns faster, so it lasts this share
  // as long (0.7 = 70%): a shield man waits for the opening behind it. Read live - a man who
  // takes his shield off his back, or puts it away for a two-handed axe, burns at the other
  // rate from then on. 1 = the same with or without.
  // (range 0.1 to 2.0)
  "FeverShieldDurationMultiplier": 0.7,

  // A man in his fever may sometimes sprint at an enemy a few metres away (the distances
  // below): every check he rolls the chance below, scaled by how much fever he has left. He
  // spends only what he holds above his order's sprint reserve (the Sprint settings). Needs the
  // sprint and "AI soldiers sprint on new orders" on.
  // (true or false)
  "FeverSprintToClose": true,

  // A fever sprint starts only with the nearest enemy at least this far away - closer is
  // already melee.
  // (range 0.0 to 10.0)
  "FeverSprintMinMetres": 3.0,

  // A fever sprint starts only with an enemy within this many metres. It ends by itself once an
  // enemy is within a few metres.
  // (range 1.0 to 40.0)
  "FeverSprintMaxMetres": 12.0,

  // How often a feverish man who could sprint at an enemy rolls for it, in seconds.
  // (range 0.1 to 10.0)
  "FeverSprintCheckSeconds": 1.0,

  // The chance of a fever sprint at each roll at the fever's start (and while it waits for the
  // first enemy); it falls in a straight line with the fever left - half the fever left, half
  // the chance; empty, none. Several sprints per fever are possible, rarer as it fades. 0 =
  // never.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "FeverSprintChancePercent": 50,

  // ==================== Courage and cohesion (AI) ====================
  // The circle a man weighs his mates and his enemies in. Men within half of it count in full,
  // then less and less to 0 at its edge (so a man walking across the edge does not flip
  // anybody's courage at once).
  // (range 2.0 to 30.0)
  "CourageRadiusMetres": 6.0,

  // How strong his side must be around him, in percent of the enemy's strength there, for a
  // fresh, steady soldier to stay brave: 100 = even odds. Tiredness, low morale and shock raise
  // it; a fever and being a hero lower it. Lower = braver soldiers.
  // (range 10 to 500)
  "CourageBaseOddsPercent": 100,

  // How much armour makes a man count: his toughness is x (1 + this % x his armour / 100) -
  // head, body, legs and arms added up. At 200 a looter in rags (armour ~8) counts 1.16, a man
  // in 110 armour 3.2.
  // (range 0 to 500)
  "CourageArmourWeightPercent": 200,

  // How much a man's level (his troop tier - his skill and experience) makes him count: x (1 +
  // this % x level / 30), levels above 60 count as 60. At 100 a level-5 looter counts x1.17, a
  // level-26 legionary x1.87. With the armour: about 4-5 looters per armoured veteran.
  // (range 0 to 500)
  "CourageLevelWeightPercent": 100,

  // A wounded man counts less: x (1 - this % x the share of his health he lost). At 50 a man at
  // half health counts 75%.
  // (range 0 to 100)
  "CourageHealthWeightPercent": 50,

  // An ENEMY who carries a two-handed weapon or a polearm and no shield counts this much more
  // (150 = half again): a great axe breaks a guard and outreaches a sword - tired, shaken men
  // will not stay against three or four such shock troops. Read once from his kit. 100 = no
  // difference.
  // (range 100 to 500)
  "CourageHeavyWeaponPercent": 150,

  // A tired man clings to his mates: the odds he needs x (1 + this % x the share of his
  // Athletics bar he has spent). At 100 an empty man needs twice the odds of a fresh one. Not
  // counted while the Athletics system is off.
  // (range 0 to 500)
  "CourageTiredWeightPercent": 100,

  // The game's own morale (0-100; men start at 35-65 and lose it as their side falls): below 50
  // the odds he needs x (1 + this % x (50 - morale) / 50). At 100 a man at morale 25 needs 1.5
  // times the odds. High morale adds nothing - that is the fever's job.
  // (range 0 to 500)
  "CourageMoraleWeightPercent": 100,

  // A man who was hit is SHOCKED for this many seconds: each landed hit in that window counts
  // (and, once the impact system's effects are built, each stagger, knockdown or dropped
  // shield), three = full shock. A shocked man needs higher odds, wants more mates around him
